        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "@wroyca, I've only now noticed that you use 'CKolkey/neogit' while 'mini.base16' explicitly states that it [supports 'TimUntersberger/neogit'](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/4fd0f9c72fb54696442c81a64c71514c95239148/doc/mini-base16.txt#L51). I can see that there are a lot of opened issues and PRs in 'TimUntersberger/neogit', but it doesn't look like a completely abandoned project.\r\n\r\nThere doesn't seem to be an issue with 'TimUntersberger/neogit'.\r\n\r\n> I discovered that modifying nvim's colorscheme after opening Neogit results in colors being completely absent - I believe that Mini is likely overriding highlights that Neogit relies upon?\r\n\r\nI think the issue is quite opposite. When you load color scheme, it usually does `highlight clear` to delete all highlight groups prior to it and then defines its own. 'mini.base16' (and 'mini.hues' for that matter) doesn't define own highlight groups and relies on good defaults from 'neogit' repo (which are good enough).\r\n\r\nCommit which your pointed out in CKolkey/neogit#45 has changes which remove `highlight default link` calls to define default highlighting. This is the correct way for plugin to define own persistent defaults for highlighting.\r\n\r\nSo I'll close this issue as it doesn't exactly come from 'mini.base16' while suggesting at least these solutions:\r\n- Make 'CKolkey/neogit' use proper `hi default link` calls to create default highlighting.\r\n- Apply 'mini.base16' color scheme before loading 'CKolkey/neogit'.\r\n- Use 'TimUntersberger/neogit'.",
